WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.701
Picture the storeroom behind a small fruit shop.

2
00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:10.515
Boxes sit on the shelves, each with a label: Gala apples, lemons, paper bags.

3
00:00:10.515 --> 00:00:14.141
Someone in the back room fills them before the shop opens.

4
00:00:14.141 --> 00:00:16.892
The person at the counter trusts the labels.

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.039
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:22.658
Why label a box at all?

7
00:00:22.658 --> 00:00:25.967
A box that takes anything sounds more flexible.

8
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:28.080
In Kotlin, that box holds Any.

9
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.206
In Java, Object.

10
00:00:29.206 --> 00:00:30.896
So what's wrong with it?

11
00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:32.913
Let's find out.

12
00:00:32.913 --> 00:00:37.707
One morning, the counter opens the box for today's apple, to write the sign.

13
00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:40.798
Inside, where a name should be, is the number 12.

14
00:00:40.798 --> 00:00:44.772
The back room wrote how many apples there were, not which kind.

15
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.807
Notice where things happened.

16
00:00:46.807 --> 00:00:48.863
The mistake was made in the back.

17
00:00:48.863 --> 00:00:50.545
It shows up at the counter.

18
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:52.752
Here it is in code.

19
00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:54.984
AnyBox holds a value of type Any.

20
00:00:54.984 --> 00:00:57.690
restock is the back room: it puts 12 in.

21
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:02.628
signFor is the counter: it takes the value out, and casts it to a String.

22
00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:05.064
A cast tells the compiler: trust me.

23
00:01:05.064 --> 00:01:08.040
The real check waits until the program runs.

24
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:10.365
So where does this program fail?

25
00:01:10.365 --> 00:01:13.446
On the line that puts 12 in, or at the counter?

26
00:01:13.446 --> 00:01:14.495
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:21.090
Here's what the lesson's AnyBoxDemo printed.

28
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.080
restock returned, and nothing has failed yet.

29
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.867
Then a ClassCastException, thrown in signFor, at line 13.

30
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:29.927
That's the cast at the counter.

31
00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:35.007
The counter did nothing wrong, and the function with the bug isn't even in the trace.

32
00:01:35.007 --> 00:01:38.104
In a bigger program, that read could come hours later.

33
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:42.408
If Any had an honest name, it would be called whatever.

34
00:01:42.408 --> 00:01:47.132
It says yes to everything, and leaves the bad news for whoever opens the box.

35
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:48.560
So the point is this.

36
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.254
Any doesn't remove the check.

37
00:01:50.254 --> 00:01:53.117
It moves the check to the read, far from the bug.

38
00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:58.667
What we really want is a check at the storeroom door, on the line that puts the wrong thing in.

39
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.059
So let's print a label with a gap in it.

40
00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:03.595
Box of, and then a blank.

41
00:02:03.595 --> 00:02:07.283
The person who makes the box has no idea what will go in it.

42
00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:10.848
Whoever takes the box writes in the gap: apple names, say.

43
00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:14.891
And at the storeroom door stands a clerk who reads every label.

44
00:02:14.891 --> 00:02:17.137
A name like Fuji fits, so it goes in.

45
00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:20.900
The number 12 doesn't match, so it's turned away, at the door.

46
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:21.998
Let's map it.

47
00:02:21.998 --> 00:02:25.791
The printed label is class Box, with T in angle brackets.

48
00:02:25.791 --> 00:02:26.922
The gap is the T.

49
00:02:26.922 --> 00:02:31.513
What you write in the gap is a real type, like String in Box&lt;String&gt;.

50
00:02:31.513 --> 00:02:34.308
And the clerk at the door is the compiler.

51
00:02:35.233 --> 00:02:38.654
Now give restock a Box&lt;String&gt;, and try the same move.

52
00:02:38.654 --> 00:02:44.356
The compiler stops it right there, on the line with the bug, before the program ever runs.

53
00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:48.931
kotlinc, the Kotlin compiler, says: assignment type mismatch.

54
00:02:48.931 --> 00:02:52.079
Actual type is Int, but String was expected.

55
00:02:52.079 --> 00:02:57.088
And javac, the Java compiler, says: int cannot be converted to String.

56
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.644
The counter needs no cast any more.

57
00:02:59.644 --> 00:03:05.804
The value in a Box&lt;String&gt; is already a String, so signFor can't throw a ClassCastException.

58
00:03:06.733 --> 00:03:09.302
Here's a trick that predicts any call.

59
00:03:09.302 --> 00:03:14.238
The box gets a method, replace: put a new value in, get the old one back.

60
00:03:14.238 --> 00:03:18.565
We make two boxes: variety, holding Gala, and count, holding 12.

61
00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:21.472
Will count dot replace, with Fuji, compile?

62
00:03:21.472 --> 00:03:22.553
Pause and guess.

63
00:03:25.533 --> 00:03:26.768
Fill in the blank.

64
00:03:26.768 --> 00:03:30.266
count is a Box&lt;Int&gt;, so read every T in Box as Int.

65
00:03:30.266 --> 00:03:34.312
For this call, replace takes an Int, and gives back an Int.

66
00:03:34.312 --> 00:03:36.781
Fuji is a String, so it doesn't fit.

67
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.197
The compiler agrees: argument type mismatch.

68
00:03:40.197 --> 00:03:43.393
Actual type is String, but Int was expected.

69
00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:49.351
The same call on variety is fine, and the demo prints: variety was Gala, now Fuji.

70
00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:54.926
So one class gives you many types: Box&lt;String&gt;, Box&lt;Int&gt;, Box&lt;Apple&gt;.

71
00:03:54.926 --> 00:03:58.707
To the compiler, they're as different as String and Int.

72
00:03:58.933 --> 00:04:01.116
Functions can have a blank too.

73
00:04:01.116 --> 00:04:03.791
In Kotlin, the T goes right after fun.

74
00:04:03.791 --> 00:04:06.045
In Java, before the return type.

75
00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:10.058
A list of apples goes in, and an apple or null comes out.

76
00:04:10.058 --> 00:04:11.255
No cast anywhere.

77
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:13.032
Now the names.

78
00:04:13.032 --> 00:04:16.306
T is called a type parameter: the blank on the label.

79
00:04:16.306 --> 00:04:20.012
The real type you write in, like String, is a type argument.

80
00:04:20.012 --> 00:04:26.252
A class or function with a blank is generic, and that's where this track's name comes from: generics.

81
00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:30.619
The picture breaks in one place, and it matters later.

82
00:04:30.619 --> 00:04:32.537
A real label stays on the box.

83
00:04:32.537 --> 00:04:35.925
The compiler checks the label, and then tears it off.

84
00:04:35.925 --> 00:04:41.807
When the program runs, on the JVM, there is only one Box class, and the String part is gone.

85
00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:48.519
So our clerk is strict at the door, with one odd habit: every label goes in the bin before the shop opens.

86
00:04:48.519 --> 00:04:51.945
That's fine, because by then every box has been checked.

87
00:04:52.867 --> 00:04:53.683
Three rules.

88
00:04:53.683 --> 00:04:58.783
One: a cast like as String, on a value from Any, marks a box with no label.

89
00:04:58.783 --> 00:05:02.047
Two: don't widen a type to Any to feel flexible.

90
00:05:02.047 --> 00:05:04.156
Here, flexible means unchecked.

91
00:05:04.156 --> 00:05:08.644
Three: a plain T promises nothing, and the next lesson fixes that.

92
00:05:09.567 --> 00:05:11.448
So, why put a label on the box?

93
00:05:11.448 --> 00:05:14.604
Because a cast checks at the read, far from the bug.

94
00:05:14.604 --> 00:05:19.763
A type parameter puts the check at the write, on the very line that made the mistake.

95
00:05:19.967 --> 00:05:20.787
Quick check.

96
00:05:20.787 --> 00:05:25.502
Someone writes the line on the screen: a Box&lt;String&gt;, filled with 42.

97
00:05:25.502 --> 00:05:27.347
Where do you hear about it?

98
00:05:30.533 --> 00:05:38.262
Right on that line, before the program runs: initializer type mismatch, expected Box&lt;String&gt;, actual Box&lt;Int&gt;.

99
00:05:38.262 --> 00:05:40.019
The clerk read the label.
